# ---------------- Chapter 22: device technologies at the load line
NEW[22] = [
('H2', '22.4 Device technologies at the load line'),
('P', "The classes above describe what the transistor does in each cycle; the technology decides how far it can swing while doing it. Every power amplifier in this book starts from the same load line, Ropt = (V − Vknee)²/(2P), whatever the device, so the technologies differ in the numbers that go into it: the supply the device survives, the current per millimeter of gate or square micrometer of emitter, the frequency at which it still has gain, and the heat it can shed. CMOS comes first here, because it is the technology of nearly every integrated radio, and the other families are described by what changes. Why each material behaves as it does is Book 3's subject, in its chapter " + q("Materials for Speed, Power, and Heat") + "; Part III of this book designs with each of them."),
('P', "The comparison is given as rows, one per technology, each in the same order: breakdown and supply, power density, speed, efficiency and linearity, cost and integration, and typical uses. The figures are typical of commercial processes in 2026 and vary by a factor of two or more between processes."),
('P', "Bulk CMOS and RF SOI: thin-oxide core devices tolerate about 1 V and thick-oxide input-output devices 1.8 to 3.3 V, so a battery-powered stage needs cascodes or stacks, Chapter 42; current density is set by gate width, and power is limited by voltage rather than current; fT and fmax reach a few hundred gigahertz in advanced nodes; efficiency is moderate, 30 to 50 % for linear Wi-Fi stages at average power; the PA shares a die with the transceiver and the processor, which is the decisive advantage; Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, IoT radios such as the ESP32, and integrated millimeter-wave beamformers."),
('P', "SiGe HBT: a fast silicon-germanium bipolar device in a BiCMOS process; high-speed devices with fT near 300 GHz and fmax near 500 GHz have a BVCEO of only about 1.5 V, while slower power variants tolerate several volts; very high gain per stage at millimeter waves; automotive radar at 77 GHz, optical transceivers, and millimeter-wave front ends, Chapter 46."),
('P', "GaAs HBT (InGaP emitter): a collector breakdown BVCEO of about 15 to 20 V lets it run from a 3.4 to 4.5 V battery with wide margin for mismatch; fT of a few tens of gigahertz; good linearity and efficiency at a few watts; a separate die in a front-end module; the cellular handset power amplifier, Chapter 44."),
('P', "GaAs pHEMT: a high-electron-mobility FET with very low noise, about 0.5 dB at 2 GHz in a packaged part, and low switch loss; power density near 1 W/mm; LNAs, switches, and microwave MMICs, Chapters 28 and 48."),
('P', "GaN HEMT on silicon carbide or silicon: supplies of 28 to 50 V, power densities of about 4 to 6 W/mm at 28 V and up to about 10 W/mm at 50 V in commercial processes, and channel temperatures rated to about 225 °C; efficient and broadband at the high impedance its voltage gives; depletion-mode, so it needs a negative gate and a bias sequence; base stations, radar, satellite amplifiers, and broadband test amplifiers, Chapter 45."),
('P', "LDMOS: a laterally diffused silicon MOSFET for 28 to 50 V supplies, rugged and cheap, with useful gain to about 3 to 4 GHz; broadcast, industrial heating, and older base stations, Chapter 20."),
('EQ', "Ropt = (V − Vknee)² / (2P),    Ropt,2 / Ropt,1 = [(V₂ − Vk₂) / (V₁ − Vk₁)]²  at equal power"),
('WE', 'Worked Example 22.3', "Problem. Find Ropt for a 1 W stage in four technologies: a CMOS cascode on 3.3 V with a 0.5 V knee, a GaAs HBT on 3.4 V with a 0.3 V knee, and GaN on 28 V with a 3 V knee and on 48 V with a 4 V knee. Find the single-section L-network Q to 50 Ω for each, and the ratio of the GaN and CMOS loads.",
 "Solution. CMOS: Ropt = 2.8²/2 = 3.92 Ω, a ratio of 12.8 to 50 Ω and Q = √(12.8 − 1) = 3.43. GaAs HBT: Ropt = 3.1²/2 = 4.81 Ω and Q = 3.07. GaN at 28 V: Ropt = 25²/2 = 312.5 Ω, above 50 Ω, so the network transforms up, with Q = √(312.5/50 − 1) = 2.29. GaN at 48 V: Ropt = 44²/2 = 968 Ω and Q = 4.28. At equal power the GaN load at 28 V is 312.5/3.92 = 80 times the CMOS load, the square of the ratio of the voltage swings; a figure of 10 sometimes quoted would compare supplies only √10 = 3.2 times apart. A 1 W GaN device is therefore an unusual part: GaN's voltage pays off at tens to hundreds of watts, where its load comes back down near 50 Ω, while a 1 W handset stage on a battery needs a large step-down ratio."),
('FIG', 'b4s_ropt_tech.png', "Figure 22.2 — Above: the optimum load against output power for five technologies at their usual supplies and knees, from Ropt = (V − Vknee)²/(2P); the 50 Ω line shows where each needs no transformation. Below: output curves of a FET (square-law transfer, knee near 0.5 V) and an HBT (exponential transfer, knee near 0.3 V) on the same axes, each with its class B load line for 0.5 W from 3.3 V.",
 "Two panels. A log-log plot of optimum load resistance versus power for CMOS 3.3 V, GaAs HBT 3.4 V, LDMOS 28 V, GaN 28 V and GaN 48 V, with a 50 ohm line; and output characteristics of a FET and an HBT with load lines."),
('P', "Bipolar version. Where the rest of this book designs a CMOS or FET stage, a bipolar stage changes in five ways, and each design in Part III closes with a short note on them."),
('B', "Drive and bias: an HBT is driven by base current, with collector current exponential in VBE; a FET is driven by gate voltage with a roughly square-law, and in short channels nearly linear, transfer. An HBT needs a current mirror or ballasted bias, a FET a gate voltage."),
('B', "Breakdown and swing: SiGe and GaAs HBTs tolerate more collector swing than thin-oxide CMOS, so they need less stacking; BVCBO, with the base driven from a low impedance, is the limit that matters under drive, and it is well above BVCEO."),
('B', "Knee: VCE,sat of 0.2 to 0.3 V is comparable to or below a FET's knee, and the load-line formula is the same."),
('B', "Linearity: the exponential base-emitter junction gives different AM-AM and AM-PM curves, and HBT stages often need less predistortion at moderate back-off."),
('B', "Heat: an HBT's VBE falls about 1 to 2 mV per kelvin at constant current, so hot fingers hog current; multi-finger HBTs need emitter or base ballast, Chapter 44, while FETs share current more evenly because their mobility falls with temperature."),
('KEY', "Key idea. One load line serves every technology; what changes is the voltage it may swing. At equal power the optimum load scales with the square of that swing, which is why a battery-powered stage must transform down by a large ratio and a 48 V GaN stage meets 50 Ω only at hundreds of watts."),
]
# ---------------- Chapter 28: a pHEMT LNA from a real data sheet
NEW[28] = [
('H2', '28.5 A pHEMT LNA from a real data sheet'),
('P', "The noise parameters of Section 28.1 are published for real devices, usually in a table and in a Touchstone file whose format Chapter 48 explains. The Broadcom ATF-54143, an enhancement-mode GaAs pHEMT in a four-lead plastic package, is a long-lived LNA part whose data sheet gives them at 3 V and 60 mA. At 2.0 GHz it lists NFmin = 0.45 dB, Γopt = 0.27∠120.1°, and a normalized noise resistance of 0.04, so Rn = 0.04 × 50 = 2.0 Ω, with an associated gain of 18.29 dB at Γopt. Its S-parameters at the same bias give S11 = 0.62∠−168.3°, S21 = 7.31∠73.3°, S12 = 0.06∠33.3°, and S22 = 0.17∠−117.0°. The reference planes are the ends of the leads on the vendor's alumina test carrier with four source vias, so a board with longer source vias will not reproduce these numbers."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 28.3', "Problem. Using the ATF-54143 data at 2.0 GHz, find the noise figure with a 50 Ω source and at Γopt, and decide how precisely the input match must hit Γopt. Then find Rollett's K and say what it implies for the design.",
 "Solution. With Γs = 0: |Γs − Γopt|² = 0.0729 and |1 + Γopt|² = |0.865 + j0.234|² = 0.803, so F = 1.109 + (4 × 2.0/50) × 0.0729/0.803 = 1.109 + 0.0145 = 1.124, a noise figure of 0.51 dB, only 0.06 dB above NFmin. The small Rn, 2.0 Ω against Worked Example 28.1's 8 Ω, makes the device forgiving: the input match can favor return loss and stability with little noise penalty. K computes to 0.81 at 2 GHz, below 1, so the device is only conditionally stable; the data sheet accordingly quotes the maximum stable gain, MSG = |S21|/|S12| = 20.86 dB, not a MAG. The design needs stabilization, typically a small resistance in the drain feed and the source inductance of the vias, before the input network is drawn. Chapter 48 does the rest: reading the file, plotting noise circles, and designing the match."),
('PL', 'Where this continues. ', "Chapter 48 reads the same device's Touchstone file, computes K, μ, and MSG across frequency, draws the noise and gain circles, and designs an L-section that presents Γopt from 50 Ω. Noise parameters themselves are measured with a tuner and a noise-figure analyzer, as " + B9 + ", describes in its chapter " + q("Noise Figure and Phase Noise Analyzers") + "."),
]
# ---------------- Chapter 41: the rest of the alphabet, operating modes, which class for which job
NEW[41] = [
('H2', '41.12 The rest of the alphabet, and which class for which job'),
('P', "Three groups organize every amplifier letter in use. The conduction-angle classes, A, AB, B, and C, keep the transistor a current source and choose how much of the cycle it conducts, Section 41.1 and Chapter 22. The switching and harmonic-tuned classes, D, E, F, inverse F, J, inverse J, and S, shape the voltage and current so that they do not overlap, Sections 41.2 to 41.5 and Chapter 35. The efficiency-enhancement architectures, the Doherty, Chireix outphasing, envelope tracking, and Kahn's envelope elimination and restoration, are not classes at all but ways of combining amplifiers or moving their supply, Sections 41.5, 41.6, and 41.9 and Chapters 23 and 36. Figure 41.2 draws the drain waveforms of the main classes on one scale."),
('P', "Inverse class J is the dual of class J: the current waveform carries the reactive second-harmonic shaping and the voltage approaches a half sine, which suits devices whose current, not voltage, is the tighter limit. Cripps and his colleagues developed the continuous class B/J family in the mid-2000s, and its inverse and continuous inverse class F forms followed; they are the working basis of most broadband GaN amplifiers and appear in CMOS research designs. Class T is not a physical class: it was Tripath's trademark for a digitally controlled switching audio amplifier, a class D variant. Classes G and H, the rail-switching and rail-tracking classes of Section 41.5, began as audio techniques and are the audio cousins of envelope tracking; audio amplifiers are outside this book."),
('P', "The operating mode cuts across the classes. A saturated amplifier is driven until its output stops rising, which is efficient and acceptable only for constant-envelope signals such as FM, GFSK, and LoRa chirps. A linear amplifier stays below compression for signals with a varying envelope. Deep class AB biases close to class B for efficiency and leans on predistortion for linearity, and most modern communications amplifiers run in soft compression at their peaks, with crest-factor reduction and predistortion keeping the spectrum inside its mask."),
('P', "Which class for which job. The choice follows the signal, the power, and the frequency more than any preference."),
('B', "Wi-Fi and Bluetooth in an ESP32-class chip: a CMOS class AB stage, backed off for OFDM and run nearer saturation for Bluetooth's constant envelope, Chapter 42."),
('B', "LoRa and sub-gigahertz IoT radios: constant-envelope chirps allow a saturated class AB, E, or F stage at +14 to +22 dBm; the efficiency gained shows up as battery life."),
('B', "Radio-control transmitters at 2.4 GHz: frequency-hopping GFSK, constant envelope, a saturated CMOS stage."),
('B', "Wireless charging: class E or class D inverters at 6.78 MHz for magnetic-resonance chargers, and half- or full-bridge class D inverters between about 100 and 200 kHz for Qi pads, Chapter 43."),
('B', "Audio: class D, with G and H in linear designs."),
('B', "Cellular handsets: GaAs HBT class AB modules with average-power or envelope tracking, Chapter 44."),
('B', "Base stations: GaN Doherty amplifiers with digital predistortion, Chapter 45; broadband test and military amplifiers in continuous class B/J."),
('B', "Radar and satellites: pulsed class AB or C GaN for radar, and GaN solid-state amplifiers or traveling-wave tubes for satellite downlinks, Chapters 32 and 45."),
('P', "In modern RF CMOS for Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, LTE, and 5G, the classes that matter most are AB, C as the Doherty peaking device, E, F and inverse F, J, the Doherty with class AB main devices, and the envelope-tracked class AB stage. Class J, inverse F, and the Doherty dominate much current research, because they balance power, efficiency, linearity, and bandwidth better than any single class."),
('FIG', 'b4s_waveforms.png', "Figure 41.2 — Idealized drain voltage (blue) and current (red) over two cycles for classes A, B, C (120°), D, E, F, inverse F, and J, each normalized to VDD and to its own peak current. Overlap of voltage and current is dissipation; the switching and harmonic-tuned classes avoid it by shaping, and class C by conducting briefly.",
 "Eight small panels of voltage and current waveforms for amplifier classes A, B, C, D, E, F, inverse F and J."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 41.10', "Problem. A battery-powered node transmits LoRa at +22 dBm and Wi-Fi OFDM at +17 dBm average with an 8 dB peak-to-average ratio. For LoRa, compare a saturated stage at 60 % drain efficiency with a linear class AB stage held 3 dB below compression at an ideal class B efficiency. For Wi-Fi, find the ideal class B efficiency and DC power at the average output.",
 "Solution. LoRa: 22 dBm is 158 mW. Saturated at 60 %, the stage draws 158/0.60 = 264 mW. Held 3 dB back, a class B stage reaches (π/4) × 10^(−3/20) = 55.6 % ideal, so 285 mW, and a practical AB stage at back-off does worse; saturation is free here because the chirp has a constant envelope. Wi-Fi: 17 dBm is 50 mW, and at 8 dB back-off the ideal class B efficiency is (π/4) × 10^(−8/20) = 31.3 %, so 160 mW of DC power for 50 mW of RF. The OFDM link costs a lower output a larger fraction of its supply, which is why the Wi-Fi side of such chips uses average-power tracking and why their LoRa and Bluetooth sides saturate."),
('KEY', "Key idea. Group the letters by mechanism: conduction angle, waveform shaping, or architecture. Then choose by the signal: saturate constant envelopes, back off or load-modulate varying ones, and let predistortion buy back linearity where efficiency has been taken."),
]
