# ---------------- On the Job notes for existing chapters
JOB = {
16: "Antenna and OTA test engineers run anechoic chambers and compact ranges, calibrate them with standard-gain horns, and measure the radiated power and sensitivity of finished phones and IoT devices for carriers and regulators. The work mixes RF measurement, mechanical fixturing, and statistics: a chamber's uncertainty budget is often ±1 dB, and knowing which part of it comes from the positioner, the cable, or the reference antenna is the job. Employers are test labs, handset and module makers, and chamber vendors; the path in is often an RF technician or test role, with Python for automation.",
25: "RF systems engineers own the line-up: the spreadsheet of gain, noise figure, and intercept that this chapter builds, the frequency plan of Chapter 29, and the link budget of Chapter 30. They negotiate with the circuit designers who own each block and the standards that set the targets, and they are the first people asked why a prototype misses its sensitivity. The job rewards fluency in decibels, a feel for which stage limits what, and the habit of writing every assumption down. It is found at chip, module, and radio makers and in defense and satellite companies.",
37: "PA characterization engineers measure what the designers promised: load-pull contours, efficiency and PAE across supply and temperature, AM-AM and AM-PM, ACLR and EVM with real signals, and ruggedness into high VSWR. Much of the work is automation, sequencing instruments in Python or a test executive, and much is skepticism, finding the cable loss or the calibration step that made a number look too good. The role sits at semiconductor companies, module makers, and base-station vendors, and it is a common route into PA design.",
}
